or of universal health care she wants  to put workers on boards higher minimum  wage  and so forth I'm not sure there's room  for both her and senator Sanders  ultimately I think they're competing  against each other they will draw votes  from each other  you've got senator Bernie Sanders from  Vermont familiar figure to many people  from his run in the 2016 Democratic  primary where he was beaten by Hillary  Clinton  he's a self-avowed socialist something  that until quite recently was pretty  rare in the Democratic Party he's been  in favor of a lot of progressive  policies like universal health care long  before they became fashionable and  that's given him a certain amount of  credibility but science is still seen as  being a big risk as being too left-wing  for a lot of Americans or at least for a  lot of the states that a Democratic  presidential candidate would have to win  to have a realistic chance of getting to  the White House but I'm sure he'll be in  the race right to the end  Pete Bhoots judges the mayor of South  Bend Indiana he's doing well in the  polls at the moment he's popular  particularly among well-educated sort of  urban Democrats if you told me before  2016 that the mayor of a small  Midwestern town would be not only  running for president but would be in  with a shot of getting one of the major  party's nominations I would have thought  you were nuts and tried to sell you some  real estate but Donald Trump changed all  the rules boost Church is doing rather  well he has lots of policies for all  sorts of things one of the most striking  ones to my mind is that he'd like to put  an extra few justices on the Supreme  Court to try and counterbalance the  conservative justices who have been  placed there in the past couple of years  Kamla Harris is a Democratic senator  from California before she was a senator  she was prosecutor she's an interesting  candidate for a number of reasons if Joe  Biden slips up in some way during this  primary then who will benefit from that  it looks at the moment that Kamala  Harris might be the best place person to  benefit she's in a similar sort of Lane  in the party to Joe Biden but she has a  couple of other things going for her she  would be the first African American  woman to get the nomination so her  kandacy would have a certain kind of  history  dimension early in her candidacy she  attached herself to a lot of policies  from the progressive wing of the party  where it seemed like that was where the  energy really was before Joe Biden  stepped in so she said she was in favor  of Medicare for all for instance  debt-free college raise in the minimum  wage and so on one challenge for her is  relative to Joe Biden and Bernie Sanders  she's really not particularly well known  around America then there's a whole  group of candidates behind that leading  group just to mention just a highlight a  smattering of the best ones better rorke  former congressman from El Paso border  town in southwest Texas he's best known  for having run Ted Cruz closed in 2018  when he ran for the Senate and he was  such a charismatic campaigner  that he attracted a lot of political  attention nationally a lot of donations  and his presidential primary run is kind  of a springboard from from that there's  Amy Klobuchar  the senator from Minnesota she's from  the moderate part of the party and the  strongest point in her favor really is  that she does well in the Midwest where  she's from  she out polled Hillary Clinton  substantially in her home state in  Minnesota when she was up for  re-election so if you were looking for a  woman at the top of the ticket and  somebody who might be able to win back  those key states in the Midwest that  Democrats lost to Republicans in 2016  then she might be a good place to look  there's Cory Booker Democratic senator  from New Jersey he's long been seen as a  rising star in the party he's got an  interesting record actually as mayor of  Newark and New Jersey particular as an  education reformer he tends not to talk  about that very much now because some of  the things he did annoyed teachers  unions who are an important constituency  in the Democratic primary but you know  he's somebody to take seriously as well  these primaries will partly be a  referendum on what sort of party the  Democratic Party is these days  ideologically there are a lot of people  who think that the party's moved to the  left if they're right about that then  you'd expect Bernie Sanders perhaps  Elizabeth Warren to do well there are a  lot of other people who argue no it  really hasn't the thing that Democrats  want most of all is to pick somebody  who's going to beat Donald Trump they're  not particularly ideological as a party  less ideological than Republicans and so  if that's the case you  look at somebody like Joe Biden Kamla  Harris perhaps Amy Klobuchar
 

